There are examples of industries solving this effectively. For instance, the tech industry has seen success with flexible scheduling and remote work arrangements, often leveraging digital tools to maintain team cohesion and productivity. Some companies have also adopted results-oriented work environments (ROWE), where employees are evaluated on their output rather than their hours worked, allowing for more flexibility in how and when work is done.

Minimizing risks without undermining the main goals of a shorter workweek involves careful planning, open communication, and continuous monitoring of the policy's impact. This includes setting clear expectations, providing training on time management and prioritization, and regularly assessing the effectiveness of the policy in achieving its intended benefits of improved productivity and employee well-being. By taking a thoughtful and adaptive approach, organizations can mitigate potential trade-offs and ensure that the introduction of flexibility enhances rather than hinders their operations.

